---
// For a trope cell whose answer record is not in this repository: what was asked, what was
// inserted, and the two mean probabilities the trope score starts from.
import { fmt, signed } from "../lib/site.js";

const { item, facet, groupLabel } = Astro.props;
const x = facet.extra;
const max = Math.max(x.group_mean_pct, x.floor_mean_pct, 1);
const w = (v) => `${Math.max(0.5, (v / max) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`;
---
<figure class="example stim">
  <figcaption>
    <span class="fig-t">What we asked</span>
    <span class="fig-d">We took the same 2,000 real short professional biographies and added one short phrase just before the first pronoun that starts a sentence. Then we asked this yes-or-no question. The single biographies and answers for this test are not published yet, so the averages stand in for an example.</span>
  </figcaption>
  <p class="ex-q"><span class="pr-k">Question</span> <q>{item.question}</q> <span class="muted">The answer that fits the stereotype: <strong>{x.trope_consistent_answer}</strong>. {item.stereotype || item.trope}</span></p>
  <div class="stim-bars">
    <div class="sb"><span class="sb-l">With <q>{x.clause.trim()}</q></span><span class="sb-bar"><i class="sb-fill cue" style={`width:${w(x.group_mean_pct)}`}></i></span><span class="sb-v">{fmt(x.group_mean_pct)}%</span></div>
    <div class="sb"><span class="sb-l">With the control edit <q>{x.floor_clause.trim()}</q></span><span class="sb-bar"><i class="sb-fill" style={`width:${w(x.floor_mean_pct)}`}></i></span><span class="sb-v">{fmt(x.floor_mean_pct)}%</span></div>
  </div>
  <p class="note">Each bar is the model's average confidence, its own probability, for the answer <strong>{x.trope_consistent_answer}</strong>, over all the biographies. With the {groupLabel} phrase in place of the control edit, that confidence moved {signed(x.shift_pts)} percentage points [{fmt(x.shift_ci[0])}, {fmt(x.shift_ci[1])}]. Naming any of the groups we tested moved it {signed(x.general_effect_pts)} points on average. The stereotype score takes away the other groups' average move, so what is left belongs to {groupLabel} alone.</p>
</figure>
